WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.330
A hard sudoku.

2
00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:05.430
Plain backtracking needs thirteen thousand

3
00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:07.410
five hundred and twenty-eight guesses.

4
00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:10.750
A constraint solver needs four.

5
00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:14.130
Same puzzle, same rules: so

6
00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:15.030
what’s the difference?

7
00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:18.960
Today: constraint programming, with

8
00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.540
domains, propagation and search.

9
00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:25.550
In constraint programming, every variable

10
00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:28.470
has a domain: the values still possible.

11
00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:31.840
A sudoku cell starts with one to nine;

12
00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:34.700
an exam starts with every slot.

13
00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:37.340
Constraints remove values that

14
00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:39.080
can’t be part of any solution.

15
00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:41.920
That removal is propagation.

16
00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:46.080
Place a five, and five vanishes from

17
00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:48.960
its row, its column and its box.

18
00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:51.930
A cell left with one value takes

19
00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:53.390
it, and the change spreads.

20
00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:56.710
In the lab’s hard sudoku, propagation

21
00:00:56.710 --> 00:00:59.530
alone takes it from twenty-seven digits to

22
00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:02.010
fifty-one, without a single guess.

23
00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:05.740
When propagation stops, search

24
00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:07.700
guesses, but cleverly.

25
00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:11.120
Fail first: choose the variable with the

26
00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:13.880
fewest values left, because that’s where

27
00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:15.580
a wrong guess shows up soonest.

28
00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:19.020
After each guess, propagate again; at a

29
00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:21.600
dead end, undo and try the next value.

30
00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:24.970
Global constraints carry a

31
00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:26.270
whole pattern at once.

32
00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:29.510
All-different over nine cells knows more

33
00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:32.010
than thirty-six separate not-equal

34
00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:34.650
rules: if three cells share only two

35
00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:36.430
values, it fails straight away.

36
00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:39.560
CP-SAT has others for routes,

37
00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:41.300
schedules and tables.

38
00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:44.950
In CP-SAT, a four-by-four

39
00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:46.550
sudoku takes a few lines.

40
00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:50.100
Every row and every column is

41
00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:53.200
all-different, and so is every box.

42
00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:55.150
Quick check!

43
00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:59.420
Only two exam slots are left, and three

44
00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:02.200
exams all clash with each other.

45
00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:06.550
What does an all-different constraint do?

46
00:02:07.790 --> 00:02:10.050
It fails at once: three exams

47
00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:11.470
can’t fit in two slots.

48
00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:15.310
And when every branch fails, the solver

49
00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:18.330
has proved there is no solution, so

50
00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:21.470
INFEASIBLE is a proof, not a shrug.

51
00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:24.610
In the exam lab, three slots are the

52
00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:27.790
fewest, because Omar’s three exams all

53
00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:28.030
clash.

54
00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:32.430
Many problems have symmetry: swap two

55
00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:35.830
exam slots, or two identical buses, and

56
00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:37.010
you get the same plan again.

57
00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:39.750
Breaking it, for example by saying

58
00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:42.870
slot one is used before slot two, can

59
00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:44.650
cut the search many times over.

60
00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:48.760
Google’s CP-SAT mixes three ideas:

61
00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:50.980
propagation from constraint

62
00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:53.760
programming, clause learning from SAT

63
00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:56.200
solvers, which remember why a branch

64
00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.860
failed, and linear relaxations.

65
00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:01.520
It has won the MiniZinc Challenge

66
00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:04.540
again and again, and it’s what most

67
00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:06.620
OR-Tools users reach for first.

68
00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:10.490
In the app, solve sudokus three ways:

69
00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:13.610
propagate then guess, propagate only,

70
00:03:14.110 --> 00:03:15.630
and plain backtracking.

71
00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:17.990
Count the guesses each one needs.

72
00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:20.080
Your turn.

73
00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:23.220
Propagation stops with three empty

74
00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:26.240
cells: one has two candidates, the

75
00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:27.460
other two have three.

76
00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:29.410
Which cell does

77
00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:31.410
fail-first search guess in?

78
00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:35.390
The one with two: fewest values, so

79
00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:37.330
a wrong guess fails fastest.

80
00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:40.530
Remember: domains shrink by

81
00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:42.790
propagation, and search guesses

82
00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:45.550
fail-first and undoes dead ends.

83
00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:48.470
Global constraints prune more.

84
00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:51.390
And INFEASIBLE is a proof.

85
00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:55.080
Try it yourself: write the four-by-four

86
00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:57.660
sudoku in CP-SAT, boxes and all.

87
00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:01.420
Then remove one given, and count the

88
00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:03.380
solutions with a solution callback.
